WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.038 --> 00:00:03.228
- [Voiceover] I know monsters aren't real,

00:00:03.228 --> 00:00:05.339
but there's an invasive
fish here in the U.S.

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:09.149
that breathes out of the
water and walks across land.

00:00:09.149 --> 00:00:11.316
What the hell is going on?

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:15.379
I called Pam Fuller and Matt Nelson.

00:00:15.379 --> 00:00:17.000
- [Lady On Phone] Hello, we're here.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.389
- [Voiceover] Both fish biologists,

00:00:18.389 --> 00:00:21.299
who study non-indigenous aquatic species

00:00:21.299 --> 00:00:22.867
'cause I want to talk to them about

00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.089
this straight up, badass fish.

00:00:25.089 --> 00:00:26.159
- [Lady On Phone] The proper name for it

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:28.450
is actually Northern Snake head.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:29.669
- [Voiceover] Thanks, Pam.

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:30.939
When the Northern Snake head was found

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:33.691
on the eastern U.S. coast in 2002,

00:00:33.691 --> 00:00:36.708
the media went crazy,
because this fish was named

00:00:36.708 --> 00:00:40.125
one of the most invasive species to date.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.259
Its origins are in Asia, but scientists

00:00:44.259 --> 00:00:45.899
aren't sure how it got here.

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:47.858
Some say it may have
escaped a fish market,

00:00:47.858 --> 00:00:51.358
like, walked off and made
it into a nearby stream

00:00:51.358 --> 00:00:54.571
where it found a new and suitable home.

00:00:54.571 --> 00:00:56.200
And this wouldn't have been hard.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.040
Here's why.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.419
If you were to make a
successful invasive species

00:00:59.419 --> 00:01:03.519
checklist, this fish
checks every single box.

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:05.539
- [Lady On Phone] Lots of
progeny, parental care,

00:01:05.539 --> 00:01:07.851
broad thermal tolerance.

00:01:07.851 --> 00:01:11.278
- [Voiceover] Meaning they make
lots of babies that survive,

00:01:11.278 --> 00:01:15.445
can live almost anywhere,
and eat almost anything.

00:01:16.962 --> 00:01:17.912
- [Man On Phone] Whatever they can

00:01:17.912 --> 00:01:19.731
fit in their mouth, they're gonna eat.

00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:21.760
- [Voiceover] And they can
breathe out of the water.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.680
How is this even possible?

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.051
Matt told me Northern
Snake heads are called

00:01:27.051 --> 00:01:28.771
obligate air breathers, which means

00:01:28.771 --> 00:01:31.381
they must breathe on
the surface periodically

00:01:31.381 --> 00:01:33.181
in order to survive.

00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:34.788
- [Man On Phone] They
have a chamber kind of

00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:37.280
in the back of their
mouth, kind of behind where

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.251
the gills are, you can think of it

00:01:39.251 --> 00:01:41.571
kind of like a very rudimentary lung,

00:01:41.571 --> 00:01:44.120
and that allows the fish to
extract oxygen out of the air.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.989
- [Voiceover] And because
they can breathe air,

00:01:45.989 --> 00:01:48.760
they can crawl out of whatever
body of water they're in

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.851
and into another, where say,
there might be more food.

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:56.184
Even if that trek takes them a few days.

00:01:57.547 --> 00:01:59.547
The Northern Snake head has
a firm hold in our rivers'

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:03.120
ecosystems, much to the
dismay of ecologists.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.792
And while fishing has helped
control its populations,

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.125
these guys are here to stay.

